1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,348
Covered call ETFs pay out monthly,

2
00:00:02,348 --> 00:00:05,600
and people say spending it
barely dents the principal.

3
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:09,169
To check, we ran a
backtest with AI-written

4
00:00:09,169 --> 00:00:11,490
Python code on real past prices.

5
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:15,610
We tested QYLD, a
Nasdaq-100 covered call ETF.

6
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:17,836
We compared it with QQQ,

7
00:00:17,836 --> 00:00:21,980
an ETF that tracks the
Nasdaq-100 without covered calls.

8
00:00:22,430 --> 00:00:26,750
In December 2013, each account
got $100,000 of principal.

9
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,420
QYLD's payouts were all
spent as soon as they came.

10
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:35,710
To keep it fair, the QQQ account sold
the same amount on the same days.

11
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:41,250
By 2026, both accounts had paid
out the same: about $110,000.

12
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:47,380
The QYLD account had about $75,000
left, 25.3% below the principal.

13
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:52,810
The QQQ account had about
$510,000 left, 6.83 times QYLD.

14
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:56,190
Same money taken out, so why such a gap?

15
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:01,470
Why, and whether another start month gives
the same result: see the video below.
